<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warrior of Hirschlanden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Krieger von Hirschlanden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warrior of Hirschlanden is a sandstone statue of a warrior, the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> life-size anthropomorphic statue north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. A work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>, probably of the 6th century BCE, it was found in 1963 beside a low barrow with 16 burials at Hirschlanden, now part of Ditzingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and is supposed to have stood on its top. Its preserved height is 1.50 m, the feet having been broken off. The figure wears a torc, a belt with a late Hallstatt dagger and a pointed hat, and its sketchy face has led to speculation that it wears a mask. It is now in the Württembergisches Landesmuseum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, with a copy at the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warrior-of-hirschlanden.jpg" alt="Warrior of Hirschlanden" width="330" height="751" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florian Hoffmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krieger_Hirschlanden_Vs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
